## 4. Key Concepts Learned

### 4.1 Separation of Concerns

Different parts of the application should have clearly defined responsibilities.

For example, Django configuration belongs in `config`, while work-order functionality belongs in `work_orders`.

### 4.2 Modular Monolith Architecture

A single backend can be organized into separate modules without requiring microservices.

This provides structure while keeping the initial system manageable.

### 4.3 Authentication Versus Authorization

Signing in successfully does not automatically give a user permission to access every record.

Authorization must consider both the user's role and the specific resource being accessed.

### 4.4 Backend Security Enforcement

Security rules must be enforced by the backend because frontend restrictions can be bypassed.

### 4.5 Test-Driven Development

Automated tests can express expected behavior before implementation.

The Red → Green → Refactor cycle helps guide incremental development and protect existing functionality.

### 4.6 Database Integrity

Some important business rules should be protected through database constraints, not only application code.

For example, a service request must not produce multiple work orders.

## 5. Challenges and Observations

### 5.1 Understanding the Role of `config`

Initially, the purpose of the Django `config` directory required clarification.

This was resolved by learning that it contains project-wide settings and routing rather than business features.

### 5.2 Distinguishing Roles from Record Ownership

A key learning point was that two users with the same role should not necessarily have access to the same records.

The technician assignment example helped clarify object-level authorization.

### 5.3 Planning Before Coding

Day 3 focused on architecture and preparation rather than implementation.

This helped establish how the system should be organized before introducing Django code.

